Apple has <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/22/apple-pauses-ai-pin-development/">reportedly</a> shelved plans to launch an AI pendant, while Meta appears to be going all out in the nascent hardware space with a new handheld AI companion. Mark Zuckerberg used his "one more thing" moment at the company's annual Connect event on Wednesday to unveil the Muse Charm, a puck about the size of an Apple Watch featuring a tamagotchi-like, interactive avatar that can be used to chat with the company's Muse AI.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/09/muse-charm-meta.jpg" alt="" width="780" height="438" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1067095" /><blockquote>"Muse Charm is a delightful and fun device built for you to talk and interact with your Muse. It brings the power of Muse with a state of the art real-time voice model into a device that fits in your pocket. <br/>
<br/>
"We packed the whole Muse experience, including the whole real-time voice and avatar stack, into something that fits on a keychain and is always available to talk to. You just go ahead and tap here in the fingerprint sensor in the corner, and you can start talking without having to unlock a phone or open an app.<br/>
<br/>
"So if you're not wearing glasses, this is going to be by far the fastest way to talk to your Muse and to show what's going on around you."<br/>
<br/>
We'll have more to share later this year."</blockquote>That's all we got about the device, which Meta is aiming to have on sale by the holiday season. <br/>
<br/>
Otherwise, Connect was mostly about the company's new Meta VR Glasses. They have a tethered puck for the storage and battery, and the headset is five times lighter than the Meta Quest 3 at 100 grams, with full-color passthrough video support and a 5K display. (For context, M5 Apple Vision Pro weighs 750–800 grams depending on the configuration.)<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/09/meta-vr-glasses.jpg" alt="" width="780" height="438" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1067107" /><br/>
Meta says the glasses will offer "cinema-grade clarity" with support for Dolby Vision and Dolby Atmos spatial audio, and they can deliver up to three hours of continuous high-resolution media playback on a single charge. <br/>
<br/>
The device runs every game available for Quest headsets, and supports Xbox Cloud Gaming. You can also connect it to a PC or Mac and control things with a virtual keyboard and trackpad on a desk. Meta VR Glasses are set to launch in the spring with a &#36;1,300 starting price.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/09/ray-ban-meta-audio-glasses.jpg" alt="" width="780" height="438" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1067097" /><br/>
Meanwhile, in an apparent bid to throw off the "pervert glasses" label, Meta also announced an audio-only version of its Ray-Ban smart glasses. The new model supports media playback and calls, and runs for up to 12 hours on a single charge, while the charging case adds up to 48 hours of battery life. Pre-orders are open and the glasses ship on October 13, starting at &#36;349.<br/>
<br/>
Meta hasn't abandoned its camera-equipped Ray-Ban smart glasses, though. Far from it. The company is refreshing its Ray-Ban Meta with a slimmer design and a button that activates Meta AI. The third-generation model is said to have a longer nine hours of battery life (versus eight on the second-gen model) and a new six-microphone array that the company says can cut out over 90 percent of background noise. The 12MP camera supports 3K video capture, with Dolby Atmos Spatial Audio support coming soon. There are multiple styles to choose from, and prices start at &#36;449.<br/>
<br/>
